# Task FEAT-001 — Ship Quick Marker v1 End-to-End Slice

## Objective
Implement a minimal, end-to-end Quick Marker v1 flow so a user can create markers from quick entry (menu bar and hotkey path), persist them locally, render them correctly on timeline, and support basic delete for mistaken markers.

## Scope In
- Wire quick-entry submission path to marker creation for both menu trigger and global hotkey trigger.
- Support marker payload with text and precise timestamp for point markers.
- Support interval marker creation (start/end) from quick entry when interval input is provided.
- Persist created markers to local storage (SQLite/local repository path already used by Chronicle).
- Ensure timeline data source/view-model includes newly created markers with correct point/interval semantics.
- Add minimal delete action path for markers and persist deletion.
- Add or update focused tests in `ChronicleTests` for these marker flows.

## Scope Out
- No redesign of menu bar UI or timeline UI.
- No rule engine/tagging changes.
- No stats aggregation changes.
- No network/telemetry/update-check additions (offline-only invariant).
- No broad refactor outside marker flow touchpoints.
- No new DB migration unless strictly required for this slice.

## Required Tests
1. Add/update unit/integration tests covering:
2. Quick entry creates a point marker with correct text + timestamp and persists it.
3. Quick entry creates an interval marker with correct start/end and persists it.
4. Newly created marker appears in timeline mapping with correct type/span.
5. Deleting a marker removes it from persistence and timeline mapping.
6. Run full suite:
```bash
xcodebuild -project Chronicle.xcodeproj -scheme Chronicle -destination 'platform=macOS' test -resultBundlePath build/TestResults/latest.xcresult
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- User can create point and interval markers via Quick Marker entry flow.
- Marker text and timestamps are persisted locally and reload correctly.
- Timeline reflects new markers correctly (point vs interval span behavior).
- Marker delete works for incorrectly added items.
- Marker-focused tests pass.
- Full test suite passes locally.
- Changes remain scoped to FEAT-001 without unrelated module refactors.

